WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.286
Mark's father parked his car. "There's a crowd forming around Old Faithful," he said.

2
00:00:07.287 --> 00:00:11.094
"The geyser is probably about to blow. Come on, Mark!

3
00:00:11.095 --> 00:00:14.216
If we hurry, we might get to see the eruption."

4
00:00:14.217 --> 00:00:21.203
Mark had never seen a geyser erupt, so he and his father had come to Yellowstone National Park for the day.

5
00:00:21.204 --> 00:00:28.214
The park was famous for its geysers and hot springs, especially the geyser known as Old Faithful.

6
00:00:28.215 --> 00:00:33.868
Mark's dad had told him that geysers are one of the most amazing things found in nature.

7
00:00:33.869 --> 00:00:40.049
A crack in the ground is filled with hot water, with bubbles and steam rising to the surface.

8
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.010
Then—often with little warning—it erupts.

9
00:00:44.011 --> 00:00:49.501
A giant burst of steam and water shoots high into the air.

10
00:00:49.502 --> 00:00:53.745
Mark and his father made it to the geyser just in time.

11
00:00:53.746 --> 00:00:58.509
"Old Faithful is about to blow!" a park ranger shouted.

12
00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:02.676
Mark felt a vibration coming from deep beneath the earth.

13
00:01:02.677 --> 00:01:12.864
There was a strange gurgling sound, and then, from a hole in the ground, steam and water shot up over forty meters into the air!

14
00:01:12.865 --> 00:01:15.728
"Whoa!" Mark cried.

15
00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:19.377
Soon the water flowed back down into the hole.

16
00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:25.013
To Mark, it sounded just like water gurgling down the drain of a sink.

17
00:01:25.014 --> 00:01:32.045
Mark had a question for the ranger. "Why is this geyser named Old Faithful?" he asked.

18
00:01:32.046 --> 00:01:36.161
"Most geysers are unpredictable," the ranger explained.

19
00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:40.292
"But Old Faithful's eruptions follow a predictable pattern.

20
00:01:40.293 --> 00:01:44.903
If it erupts for two minutes, then it erupts again in sixty minutes.

21
00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:49.498
If it erupts for five minutes, it erupts again in one hundred minutes."

22
00:01:49.499 --> 00:01:54.646
"I get it," said Mark. "Old Faithful always does the same thing!"

23
00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:58.659
"Exactly." The ranger grinned at Mark.

24
00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:03.265
Soon Mark and his father followed the park ranger to an exhibit.

25
00:02:03.266 --> 00:02:11.234
There, Mark learned that Yellowstone Park contains more than half of all the geysers and hot springs on earth.

26
00:02:11.235 --> 00:02:17.598
"What are hot springs?" Mark asked the ranger. "Are they different from geysers?"

27
00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:20.725
"Yes, they are different," the ranger replied.

28
00:02:20.726 --> 00:02:23.572
"They form because of earth's water table.

29
00:02:23.573 --> 00:02:28.880
When rain falls, some water sinks deep into the earth until it reaches a layer of rock.

30
00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:35.564
The water sits on top of the rock and forms what's called the water table—a layer of water.

31
00:02:35.565 --> 00:02:41.298
"In some places, such as Yellowstone Park, the hot core of the earth is near this water table.

32
00:02:41.299 --> 00:02:44.508
So the water gets heated to boiling hot temperatures.

33
00:02:44.509 --> 00:02:52.518
If there is a crack in the earth above the water, the water expands and forces its way up. Water then pools around the opening."

34
00:02:52.519 --> 00:03:00.158
"I see." Mark nodded. "Hot springs don't erupt. But then what makes geysers erupt?"

35
00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:06.447
"Well," the park ranger said. "A hot spring's crack, or fissure, is shaped like a pipe.

36
00:03:06.448 --> 00:03:10.863
But a geyser's fissure is wider, forming a large cavern underground.

37
00:03:10.864 --> 00:03:16.814
Hot water and steam rise and fill the cavern while the water at the top of the cavern cools off quickly.

38
00:03:16.815 --> 00:03:20.742
This cool water acts like a heavy lid on a pot of boiling water.

39
00:03:20.743 --> 00:03:23.939
Hot pressure builds up in the cavern.

40
00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.997
Suddenly all the pressure is released at once, shooting water and steam into the air."

41
00:03:29.998 --> 00:03:34.985
Mark and his father stayed a while longer, exploring the exhibit.

42
00:03:34.986 --> 00:03:42.332
They found out that Iceland, a small country in Europe, also has a large number of geysers and hot springs.

43
00:03:42.333 --> 00:03:49.716
The people living there can even use the energy from geysers and hot springs to heat their homes and generate electricity!

44
00:03:49.717 --> 00:03:55.010
"Look, Dad!" Mark pointed to a picture of a giant mud bath.

45
00:03:55.011 --> 00:04:00.330
"Sometimes the hot spring mixes with soil to form mud baths like this one.

46
00:04:00.331 --> 00:04:03.196
I bet it feels good to lay in that!"

47
00:04:03.197 --> 00:04:07.667
"Many people find these mud baths soothing," replied Mark's dad.

48
00:04:07.668 --> 00:04:10.956
"Japan is famous for its relaxing hot springs.

49
00:04:10.957 --> 00:04:18.096
Some people even believe that mud baths and hot springs can cure diseases—or at least take away some aches and pains!"

50
00:04:18.097 --> 00:04:26.389
The ranger heard them talking. "In Yellowstone Park the hot springs' water is usually too hot to touch," he said.

51
00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:30.361
"So unfortunately, people can't bathe in the hot springs here.

52
00:04:30.362 --> 00:04:33.614
But there is something special about our hot springs.

53
00:04:33.615 --> 00:04:37.219
Some forms of bacteria and algae live in them.

54
00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:43.531
These life forms are different colors and give the hot springs an unusual and beautiful appearance."

55
00:04:43.532 --> 00:04:52.880
Later Mark and his father thanked the park ranger and headed back toward their car. It was time to go home.

56
00:04:52.881 --> 00:05:01.490
"Dad, I wish we could go to one of those hot springs where you can swim and relax," Mark said. "That sounds like fun!"

57
00:05:01.491 --> 00:05:05.936
"I've heard there are hot springs like that in Canada," replied Mark's father.

58
00:05:05.937 --> 00:05:08.971
"You can sit in a hot spring high in the mountains.

59
00:05:08.972 --> 00:05:12.256
Would you like to go there on our next vacation?" he asked.

60
00:05:12.257 --> 00:05:21.922
"Yes!" Mark said, grinning. "That sounds cool . . . er, I mean, hot!"

